# Cartolas TCT BCI

Streamlit app for ingesting, classifying and reconciling BCI credit-card
statements (Nacional CLP + Internacional USD) across multiple cards and years.

## Features

- Drag in **many statements at once** — each PDF is routed to the national or
  international parser automatically by reading its header, so a mixed batch
  of cards and months works from either tab
- Statements are identified by **card last-4 + statement date + type**, not by
  filename: renaming a PDF will not sneak a duplicate in, and two different
  cards billed on the same day stay separate
- Auto-categorise by description (static rules + learned history, scoped per
  statement type)
- Reconcile the international DEUDA TOTAL to the national TRASPASO line to
  derive the bank's exchange rate, then back-fill MONTO_CLP
- Conciliación + Kame ERP tracking, with a running total of the ticked rows
- Dashboard with per-currency KPIs, spend-by-category and an uploaded-files
  table with per-statement delete
- Filter the archive by card, date range and description

## Project structure

```
app.py                          Main Streamlit app
dashboard.py                    Dashboard + uploaded-statements table
migrate.py                      One-shot, idempotent schema migration
data/
  database.py                   PostgreSQL layer (Neon), connection pool
  common.py                     Shared statement-header parsing
  categorias.py                 Allowed TIPO_GASTO values per statement type
  extractor_nacional.py         BCI national PDF parser (CLP)
  extractor_internacional.py    BCI international PDF parser (USD)
tests/
  test_parsers.py               Pure-function tests (no DB, no PDFs)
  test_pdfs.py                  End-to-end parser tests (skip without PDFs)
.streamlit/
  config.toml                   Server settings (committed)
  secrets.toml                  Passwords (gitignored — see the .example)
requirements.txt / requirements-dev.txt
runtime.txt
```

## Data model notes

- **Money is `NUMERIC`, never a float.** `real` holds only ~7 significant
  digits, which silently rounds CLP amounts; `migrate.py` converts existing
  columns via `::text::numeric` because PostgreSQL's direct `real -> numeric`
  cast itself truncates to 6 digits.
- **Dates are stored twice**: the bank's original text (`FECHA_OPERACION`,
  `MM/DD/YY`) for display, and a real `DATE` column (`FECHA_OPERACION_D`) for
  sorting and range filters.
- **Classification propagation only fills blanks** and never crosses between
  NACIONAL and INTERNACIONAL, whose category vocabularies differ.

## Local setup

```bash
python -m venv venv
source venv/bin/activate
pip install -r requirements-dev.txt

cp .streamlit/secrets.toml.example .streamlit/secrets.toml
# edit secrets.toml: app_password + supabase_db_url

python migrate.py --dry-run   # review
python migrate.py             # apply

streamlit run app.py
```

## Tests

```bash
pytest tests/ -q
```

`tests/test_pdfs.py` runs against any statements it finds in `Cartolas/` or
`~/Desktop/Bci`, or in `$BCI_PDF_DIR`. Statements are gitignored, so those
tests skip on a fresh clone.

## Streamlit Cloud deployment

1. Push this repo to GitHub.
2. Connect on share.streamlit.io and point to `app.py`.
3. Under Settings > Secrets, add:
   ```toml
   app_password = "your-password"
   supabase_db_url = "postgresql://user:password@host/dbname?sslmode=require"
   ```
4. Run `migrate.py` once against the same database before first deploy.
5. Data lives in Neon PostgreSQL and persists across redeployments.

`runtime.txt` pins Python 3.12; `requirements.txt` uses lower bounds that also
work on 3.13, so the local venv and the deploy can differ safely.

## Exchange-rate reconciliation

The international DEUDA TOTAL (USD) is transferred to the national statement as
a single TRASPASO DEUDA INTERNACIONAL (CLP) line the following month. The app:

1. Finds the international credit line whose |USD| equals a statement's DEUDA
   TOTAL (exact match — the amounts are `NUMERIC`).
2. Matches it to the national CLP line on the same date, requiring the card to
   agree when both are known.
3. Computes tasa = CLP / USD and back-fills MONTO_CLP for every transaction in
   that statement.

Matching is automatic when unambiguous; a manual dropdown is shown otherwise.
A sanity check flags any resulting rate outside 800–1100 CLP/US$.
